{
  "summary": "Refactors validation and block storage functions that can hit fatal errors to return error details and flush status to callers using util::Result and FlushResult rather than relying solely on AbortNode shutdowns. Touched functions include block writes, block index loading, coins state flushes, mempool transaction acceptance, assumeutxo snapshot activation, block connection, and chain activation. Function signatures across the validation call hierarchy are annotated with [[nodiscard]] to enforce error handling by upstream callers.",
  "problem": "Validation and block storage routines currently handle fatal errors, such as disk write failures, by calling AbortNode directly to initiate shutdown without returning error information up the call stack. This makes it difficult for libbitcoinkernel embedders and modular application code to inspect or handle failures directly, forcing callers to rely on asynchronous notification callbacks.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author PR description: 'There are many validation functions that handle failures by calling AbortNode and triggering shutdowns, without returning error information to their callers. This makes error handling in libbitcoinkernel application code difficult, because the only way to handle these errors is to register for notification callbacks.'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [
      "#25665"
    ],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "Very large refactor spanning 54 files across validation, blockstorage, node interfaces, and test fixtures. It includes commits from prerequisite PR #25665 adding util::Result failure types and InfoType extensions.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Rebased onto the latest version of base PR #25665 (incorporating CI compiler warning workarounds and static tests for result struct sizing) with no fundamental change in the PR's error propagation architecture.",
  "card": "This pull request refactors validation and block storage functions that encounter fatal errors to return detailed status and flush state via util::Result and FlushResult instead of solely calling AbortNode. Currently, fatal validation failures trigger asynchronous node shutdowns without bubbling error context up the call stack, forcing libbitcoinkernel callers to rely on notification callbacks. The author notes that direct return values significantly simplify error handling for external consumers of the kernel library while preserving existing node behavior. The PR is based on #25665.",
  "needs": [
    "rest_of_diff"
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "uncertainties": [],
  "risk": {
    "level": "medium",
    "touches": [
      "validation"
    ],
    "why": "It modifies return types and error propagation paths throughout consensus validation and block storage execution paths, where an improperly forwarded error status could suppress an abort or misreport chain state."
  }
}
